{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Gyre Edge: Prochlorococcus Meets the Nutrient Front",
    "date": "2026-07-25",
    "lesson": "The North Pacific Subtropical Gyre is bounded by transition zones where nutrient concentrations rise sharply. Inside the gyre, the tiny cyanobacterium Prochlorococcus dominates small phytoplankton biomass and grows fastest, well-adapted to oligotrophic (nutrient-poor) water. Crossing the gyre boundary, total phytoplankton biomass increases and larger cells - nanoeukaryotes and picoeukaryotes - take over as the dominant biomass contributors, tracking the near two-orders-of-magnitude jump in nutrients. Growth rate differences between inside and outside the gyre show that oligotrophic specialists like Prochlorococcus pay a growth-rate cost for their small-size efficiency advantage. Synechococcus stays scarce even where nutrients are abundant, hinting that grazing pressure - not just nutrient supply - can shape which phytoplankton lineage wins a given water mass. This level lets you play a Prochlorococcus-like founder living right at that boundary: nutrients are scarce and patchy on one side, richer on the other, and larger competitor-type cells wait at the edge.",
    "citation": "Winter, Hynes, Berthiaume (2025). Shifts in phytoplankton community structure across oceanic boundaries. doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0324466",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "North Pacific Subtropical Gyre boundary transects, underway flow cytometry 2016-2021"
  },
